  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Change of Variables with Impulses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply change-of-variables reasoning to evaluate impulse integrals by transforming the impulse argument, adjusting the scale factor, and preserving the correct sampled time.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Change of Variables with Impulses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Change of Variables with Impulses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the unit-slope sifting pattern: if the impulse is already written as $\\delta(t-t_0)$, then $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,\\delta(t-t_0)\\,dt=x(t_0)$. That is the standard form the later algebra must recover.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a linear impulse argument $\\delta(a t-b)$, first find the zero of the argument by solving $a t-b=0$. The sampled time is $t_0=b/a$, and the change of variables must preserve that time while turning the impulse into standard form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the simplest nonunit case, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,\\delta(2t-4)\\,dt$. Set $u=2t-4$, so $dt=du/2$, and rewrite the integral so the impulse becomes $\\delta(u)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,\\delta(2t-4)\\,dt &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x\\!\\left(\\frac{u+4}{2}\\right)\\delta(u)\\,\\frac{du}{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}x(2)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That same change-of-variables step gives the general positive-slope rule: the root $t_0=b/a$ is the sample point, and the slope contributes the inverse factor $1/|a|$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,\\delta(a t-b)\\,dt &= \\frac{1}{|a|}x\\!\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right) \\\\ \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,\\delta(-a t+b)\\,dt &= \\frac{1}{|a|}x\\!\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Negative slope does not change the sampled time; it only changes the algebra of the substitution, which is why the absolute value appears. The rule to carry forward is simple: find the zero, convert to unit-impulse form, divide by the slope magnitude, and evaluate the signal at that sampled time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Change of Variables with Impulses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting a Positive Linear Impulse Argument",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y(t)\\,\\delta(4t-8)\\,dt$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the linear impulse argument $4t-8$. Find its zero first: $4t-8=0$ gives $t=2$. That is the sampled time the change of variables must preserve.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the substitution $u=4t-8$, so $dt=du/4$. Then rewrite the integral so the impulse is in standard form $\\delta(u)$ and the signal is evaluated at the transformed time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y(t)\\,\\delta(4t-8)\\,dt &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y\\!\\left(\\frac{u+8}{4}\\right)\\delta(u)\\,\\frac{du}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}y(2)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the positive-slope linear impulse argument contributes the scale factor $1/4$, and the signal is sampled at the root $t=2$. The worked result is $\\frac{1}{4}y(2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Change of Variables with Impulses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Signal Factor After the Variable Change",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}(t^2+1)\\,\\delta(5t-10)\\,dt$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the linear impulse argument $5t-10$. Solve $5t-10=0$ to get the sampled time $t=2$; that is the time the signal factor must be evaluated after the change of variables.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $u=5t-10$, so $dt=du/5$ and $t=(u+10)/5$. Because the signal factor is $t^2+1$, replace $t$ inside that factor before sifting.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}(t^2+1)\\,\\delta(5t-10)\\,dt &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u+10}{5}\\right)^2+1\\right)\\delta(u)\\,\\frac{du}{5} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}(2^2+1)=1\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the slope contributes the factor $1/5$, and the transformed signal is sampled at the root $t=2$. The worked result is $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Change of Variables with Impulses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Converting a Negative Linear Impulse Argument",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,\\delta(-3t+9)\\,dt$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the negative-slope impulse argument $-3t+9$. Solve $-3t+9=0$ to get the sampled time $t=3$; the sign of the slope does not change the sample point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $u=-3t+9$, so $dt=-du/3$ and $t=(9-u)/3$. The minus sign in $dt$ is the sign bookkeeping that will turn into the absolute scale factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,\\delta(-3t+9)\\,dt &= \\int_{\\infty}^{-\\infty}x\\!\\left(\\frac{9-u}{3}\\right)\\delta(u)\\left(-\\frac{du}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x\\!\\left(\\frac{9-u}{3}\\right)\\delta(u)\\,du \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}x(3)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the negative slope still gives a positive factor $1/3$, and the signal is sampled at the same root $t=3$. The worked result is $\\frac{1}{3}x(3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Change of Variables with Impulses",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Checking Definite Integral Bounds After the Variable Change",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\int_{0}^{2}x(t)\\,\\delta(4t-2)\\,dt$ and explain why the bounds matter.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the sampled time from $4t-2=0$. Solve first: $t_0=1/2$. Because $1/2$ lies inside the interval $[0,2]$, the impulse contributes; if the sampled time were outside the interval, the definite integral would be $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $u=4t-2$, so $dt=du/4$ and $t=(u+2)/4$. The finite bounds move with the substitution, but the $\\delta$ still samples only when the transformed interval contains $u=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}x(t)\\,\\delta(4t-2)\\,dt &= \\int_{-2}^{6}x\\!\\left(\\frac{u+2}{4}\\right)\\delta(u)\\,\\frac{du}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}x\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the bounds act as an inclusion check: here $t_0=1/2$ is inside $[0,2]$, so the scaled result is $\\frac{1}{4}x(1/2)$. If the sampled time missed the interval, the answer would be $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
